{"kind":"promise","text":"Claude promises 82327e23, the current-protocol successor for S2 of the adopted lane and worktree identity design, carrying forward the work under dc7f7d11 and promise 664f3636 rather than completing it. Outcome, conditions, performer, destination and Hugh's adoption are unchanged, and planner confirms no source or authority fact moved between 86970224 and 32660749 for this stage.\n\nImplementation is done and committed on request/checkout-creation-and-lane-refs, not yet published. It carries planner's three dispositions from 6eee19d1. One configured checkout root, gitseq.checkoutRoot, read from the repository's own Git config through the closed environment and used both for creation containment and for the existing protection; unset means no boundary, so protection keeps its derived fallback and creation reports the check not established rather than refusing against a value nobody chose, which is how I read the instruction not to reuse the conservative fallback as the creation root without shipping two roots. The lane and record readback goes through the shared S1 captured read and the existing grader, so grades stay claimed, corroborated, unresolved or foreign with no second calculation, with the ref inventory extended to cover refs/gitseq/lanes under its existing bound and the layer-7 contract updated in the same head. The hook installer is removed entirely, its flag and result fields with it, because its contract is the clarification going to Hugh; the commit trailer template stays and the absence is stated in the command, its reference page, the architecture page and the note.\n\nThree things I will put to the reviewer rather than settle alone, as planner directed: where the shared settled-status vocabulary lives, since I lifted both lists into internal/workroom rather than keeping a third copy; the bounded attempt retry, eight passes borrowed from the existing claim loop, with contention distinguished from a transient refusal by re-reading the ref; and one of my own, that attempt refs accumulate, are never deleted, and now share the ref inventory's bound with branches, so a repository with thousands of stamped checkouts would eventually withhold the whole worktrees response.\n\nBefore review I owe a recut onto this successor so the implementing commit rests on it rather than on the retired predecessor, a rebase onto current main, published artifacts at every changed path, and re-anchoring of the three documentation pages onto S2's own behaviour artifacts, which could not exist while the code was being written. Then independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review, ratification, guarded landing, push, CI and cleanup. S2 makes no schema change and its artifacts report this child only; the parent #22168 stays open for S3.","body":{"branch":"request/checkout-creation-and-lane-refs","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:06d285582dc92740d93a9e0413bfb73e82327e23","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:06d285582dc92740d93a9e0413bfb73e82327e23 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:033ceaa3c91aa563b1cc51c1b776c47c90d59242, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca and 141 more"}}